= Resource Description Framework — Technical =

== Abstract graph ==
An RDF graph G is a set of RDF triples.

For stable RDF terms:

<pre>
G ⊆ Subject × IRI × Object
</pre>

with precise allowed term categories defined by the RDF version.

Set semantics means duplicate triples do not create additional graph facts.

== Interpretation ==
A simple RDF interpretation I defines:
* a nonempty universe IR;
* property denotations;
* property extensions IEXT;
* mappings for IRIs/literals.

Satisfaction is defined for triples/graphs.

Simple entailment:

<pre>
S ⊨ E
</pre>

iff every simple interpretation satisfying S also satisfies E.

== Blank nodes ==
Blank nodes behave existentially at graph scope.

Graph isomorphism can rename blank-node identifiers without changing RDF graph meaning.

Merging graphs must therefore avoid accidental blank-node collisions.

== Entailment regimes ==
Important regimes include:
* simple entailment;
* RDF entailment;
* RDFS entailment;
* datatype entailment;
* OWL regimes defined separately.

Each regime changes which conclusions follow.

== RDFS rules ==
Subclass:

<pre>
C1 rdfs:subClassOf C2
x rdf:type C1
→ x rdf:type C2
</pre>

Subproperty:

<pre>
p1 rdfs:subPropertyOf p2
x p1 y
→ x p2 y
</pre>

Domain/range infer types rather than functioning as ordinary rejection constraints.

== RDF 1.2 triple terms ==
RDF 1.2 introduces triple terms so triples can participate as terms in other triples under defined restrictions.

This supports statement-level description patterns without using legacy RDF reification exactly as before.

Applications must remain explicit about RDF version because RDF 1.2 is not simply an RDF 1.1 syntax extension.

== Datasets ==
A dataset D contains:

<pre>
(defaultGraph, {(name_i, graph_i)})
</pre>

The RDF specification does not by itself assign one universal provenance/truth semantics to named graph names.

Applications must specify how graph names are interpreted.

